  ('cf1218cc-f93f-42ab-973c-011240da3ed8'::uuid, 'I skimmed “AlphaFold2 fails to predict protein fold switching” last week, and the framing of alphafold fails is the part I would discuss first. Several of the reported gains are small enough that they could disappear under a different split. If you are open to it, we could draft a short comparison note for the reading group. Keen to see follow-up results.', NULL::uuid, '2026-06-23T11:21:02.127571+00:00'::timestamptz::timestamptz),
  ('e892c19c-92bf-4090-b2be-da9d168e1b54'::uuid, 'I think the interesting part of “Fuzzing of Embedded Systems” is less the headline number and more how it handles fuzzing. I would flag the privacy and public-policy implications of fuzzing before anyone builds on it.', NULL::uuid, '2026-07-20T02:14:02.127571+00:00'::timestamptz::timestamptz),
  ('471072d9-559c-4e1f-a340-2acb7580e7a3'::uuid, 'Good question. I read the paper a few weeks ago and it has stayed with me. In our group we used fuzzing on a small embedded systems dataset, and the gains were modest but consistent. Several of the reported gains are small enough that they could disappear under a different split. Happy to share my notes if that helps.', NULL::uuid, '2026-07-23T00:16:02.127571+00:00'::timestamptz::timestamptz),
  ('b1d02a73-69a8-431d-ab7c-a977d2d0d95c'::uuid, 'Quick follow-up to my post: I''ll keep a running list of what we learn about debates here. The comparison would be more convincing with a stronger baseline and a proper ablation of debates. There is a useful parallel with protein folding, where the role of debates is comparable.', NULL::uuid, '2026-11-11T05:44:02.127571+00:00'::timestamptz::timestamptz),
